<%# THE username_rename INTENT — rendered from layouts/application, on EVERY page.

    SAME PLACEMENT AS shared/_contest_entry_intent AND FOR THE SAME REASON. A
    wallet returns to /auth/phantom/callback, which renders studio-engine's view.
    walletOps.resume consumes the journal at take() BEFORE it looks the handler
    up, so an intent registered only on the page that STARTED the rename would
    arrive on the callback with nothing left to retry — the user approves the
    transaction, comes back, and the rename is silently gone. Registering here
    puts the handlers on every document, including the one the wallet picks.

    Registration is idempotent and guarded: a page that did not load
    solana_studio/wallet_ops.js registers nothing and throws nothing. %>
<script>
// THE username_rename INTENT — registered here, at page load, BY NAME.
//
// WHAT THIS FLOW IS. studio-engine's leveling-activity modal owns the username
// input, the Save button and the POST to accounts#update_username. For a
// phantom or self-custodied wallet that POST answers `needs_step` with an
// opaque `challenge` — a base64 partial set_username transaction — and the
// engine hands it to the app-supplied finalize_hook (window.tmUsernameFinalize,
// in shared/_alpine_factories). The hook's job is to turn that challenge into
// an opaque `proof`: the signature of the broadcast, confirmed on-chain. The
// engine then POSTs { token, proof } to accounts#confirm_username, which
// re-verifies the transaction with TxVerifier and mirrors the name to the DB.
//
// WHY IT IS AN INTENT AND NOT A CLOSURE. On the redirect transport the page is
// DESTROYED between handing the transaction to the wallet and getting it back
// signed. A closure cannot survive that, and neither can the engine modal that
// was awaiting the hook — which is the second half of what these handlers do,
// and the part that has no counterpart in the contest-entry flow. See
// tmCompleteUsernameRename below.
//
// NOT signOnly, AND THAT IS THE ONE REAL DIFFERENCE FROM contest_entry. A
// contest entry is CO-SIGNED: prepare_entry leaves the admin signer slot empty
// and the server fills it, so a wallet that broadcast it would hand Solana a
// transaction missing a required signature. build_set_username is not like
// that — the user is its ONLY signer, so the transaction is complete the moment
// the wallet signs it and a wallet that broadcasts it directly is doing
// something correct and, on a phone, faster. Declaring signOnly here would
// force a co-signing dance that has no second signer.
//
// THE PRICE OF THAT, and it is what tmCompleteUsernameRename is shaped around:
// the send strategy is no longer knowable from the flow alone. walletOps
// answers `app-broadcasts` on the inline transport ALWAYS (runInline ignores
// signOnly entirely — signingHop, which reads it, is redirect-only), and on the
// redirect transport answers whichever hop it took: `wallet-broadcasts` from
// signAndSendTransaction, `app-broadcasts` from signTransaction. Phantom's
// send-side deeplink is deprecated so Phantom falls to the latter today, but
// Solflare and Backpack broadcast for themselves. complete() must therefore
// handle BOTH, and does.
(function () {
  var S = window.SolanaStudio;
  // GUARDED like every optional capability in this app: a page that did not
  // load solana_studio/wallet_ops.js has no registry, and an absent capability
  // must not default to the permissive branch.
  if (!S || !S.walletOps || typeof S.walletOps.define !== 'function') return;

  S.walletOps.define('username_rename', {
    // Runs BEFORE the wallet is reached on the redirect transport, so
    // everything it returns must be JSON-serialisable — it is literally
    // serialised into localStorage.
    prepare: function (ctx) {
      return window.tmPrepareUsernameRename(ctx);
    },
    // Runs on the callback document, in a page that never saw prepare().
    complete: function (ctx, result, state) {
      return window.tmCompleteUsernameRename(ctx, result, state);
    }
  });
})();

// THE FINALIZE ENDPOINT, emitted from the ROUTE HELPER rather than written as a
// string. modals/_username.html.erb hands the same helper to the engine as
// `finalize_url` for the engine's own POST; this is the second emission of it,
// for the transport where the engine is not there to post. Two emissions, one
// source — rename the route and both move. A literal '/account/confirm_username'
// here would survive the rename and 404 only on a phone.
window.tmUsernameRenameFinalizeUrl = "<%= confirm_username_account_path %>";

// THE CALLER'S LEDGER — how a handler learns whether anything upstream is still
// alive to finish the flow.
//
// WHY THIS IS NEEDED HERE AND WAS NOT NEEDED FOR contest_entry. The entry flow
// posts its own confirm and owns its own outcome, so complete() does the same
// work on both transports. This flow does not: on the INLINE transport the
// engine's leveling modal is still awaiting the hook's return value and will
// POST finalize_url itself the moment it gets one — that is the engine's
// contract, unchanged by this migration. On the REDIRECT transport that modal
// died with its document, so if complete() does not post the finalize, nobody
// does: the user signs, comes back, and the rename is confirmed on-chain but
// never mirrored to the database.
//
// DOCUMENT IDENTITY IS THE DISCRIMINATOR, which is exactly what makes a plain
// window global the right instrument rather than a hack. tmUsernameFinalize
// sets this synchronously in the document it runs in. On the inline transport
// complete() executes in THAT SAME document and finds it. On the redirect
// transport complete() executes in a document that was freshly parsed after the
// wallet returned, where every window global this file did not itself set is
// undefined. There is no third case, and no timer or transport flag would
// answer the question more directly than "is my caller's window still here".
//
// The same record carries the engine's onProgress callback, for the same
// reason and with the same lifetime: it is a function, so it can never cross
// the journal, and on the far side there is no modal for it to update anyway.
window.tmUsernameRenameCaller = null;


// THE CALLBACK DOCUMENT DOES NOT HAVE window.authedFetch YET, and complete()
// runs there. authedFetch ships only in the DEFERRED importmap module
// solana_utils.js, while studio-engine's phantom_callback view dispatches from
// a bare inline script during body parse. A direct window.authedFetch(...)
// therefore throws a TypeError on the one document that matters — after the
// user has approved in their wallet, and after resume() consumed the journal.
// Identical to shared/_contest_entry_intent's tmEntryFetch, including the 401
// normalisation: authedFetch answers FALSY on a 401 having surfaced the login
// modal, plain fetch answers a 401 Response, and the caller below branches on
// `if (!resp)`.
window.tmRenameFetch = function (url, opts) {
  if (typeof window.authedFetch === 'function') return window.authedFetch(url, opts);
  return window.fetch(url, opts).then(function (resp) {
    return resp && resp.status === 401 ? null : resp;
  });
};

// AND NEITHER IS window.pollConfirmation, WHICH IS THE SAME FILE. The old
// hook called it directly; that call was safe only because the old hook could
// never run anywhere but the page that started the rename. Moving the confirm
// wait into complete() moves it onto the callback document, where
// solana_utils.js has not executed — so this is the same class of defect as
// the authedFetch one above, discovered by asking the same question of every
// global these handlers touch.
//
// Delegates to the real implementation whenever it IS loaded, so the inline
// transport keeps the polling behaviour it has today and there is only one
// implementation to tune. The fallback is deliberately the narrow case
// solana_utils' version reduces to for this flow: no observer, default
// interval and ceiling. Pure fetch + JSON-RPC, no web3.js, so it is safe to
// run during body parse.
window.tmRenamePollConfirmation = function (rpcUrl, sig) {
  if (typeof window.pollConfirmation === 'function') return window.pollConfirmation(rpcUrl, sig);

  var intervalMs = 1500;
  var deadline = Date.now() + 60000;

  function attempt() {
    if (Date.now() >= deadline) {
      return Promise.reject(new Error(
        'Confirmation timed out after 60s (the transaction may still land — check the explorer).'
      ));
    }
    return window.fetch(rpcUrl, {
      method: 'POST',
      headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
      body: JSON.stringify({
        jsonrpc: '2.0', id: 1, method: 'getSignatureStatuses',
        params: [[sig], { searchTransactionHistory: true }]
      })
    }).then(function (resp) {
      return resp && resp.ok ? resp.json() : null;
    }).then(function (out) {
      if (out && out.error) {
        throw new Error('getSignatureStatuses RPC error: ' + JSON.stringify(out.error));
      }
      var st = out && out.result && out.result.value && out.result.value[0];
      if (st) {
        if (st.err) throw new Error('Transaction failed on-chain: ' + JSON.stringify(st.err));
        if (st.confirmationStatus === 'confirmed' || st.confirmationStatus === 'finalized') return st;
      }
      return new Promise(function (r) { setTimeout(r, intervalMs); }).then(attempt);
    });
  }
  return attempt();
};

// THE RPC ENDPOINT, read from the DOM at the moment it is used rather than
// journalled. data-solana-rpc-url is on <body> in layouts/application, so it is
// present on BOTH documents this flow runs across and re-reading it costs
// nothing. Journalling it instead would have written a URL into localStorage
// for ten minutes to answer a question the page can always answer itself.
function renameRpcUrl() {
  return (document.body && document.body.dataset && document.body.dataset.solanaRpcUrl) || '';
}

// THE CSRF TOKEN — THIS DOCUMENT'S OWN FIRST, THE CALLER'S AS A FALLBACK.
//
// A Rails csrf_meta_tags token is session-bound rather than page-bound, and
// studio-engine's layouts/studio/_head emits the tag on every document here, so
// the callback page normally has a perfectly good one of its own. Reading it
// LIVE is the better primary: nothing extra has to cross the redirect, and a
// token rotated while the user was inside their wallet app still works.
//
// THE FALLBACK IS NOT DEFENSIVE PADDING — it was earned. The first cut of this
// read the meta tag and nothing else, on the reasoning above, and
// e2e/username_rename_callback_registration then posted the finalize with an
// EMPTY token. The cause turned out to be the e2e server, which disables
// forgery protection so `csrf_meta_tags` emits nothing at all (measured: the tag
// is absent on `/` there too, not just on the callback). That is an environment
// and not a defect — but it is the same shape as any document that ships without
// the tag, and an empty header would 422 a rename that is already on-chain and
// cannot be retried. So the caller's token rides in ctx the way
// shared/_contest_entry_intent has always carried its own, and this reaches for
// it only when the live tag has nothing to give.
function renameCsrfToken(ctx) {
  var meta = document.querySelector('meta[name="csrf-token"]');
  return (meta && meta.content) || (ctx && ctx.csrfToken) || '';
}

// Prepare: convert the engine's opaque challenge into the ONE shape walletOps
// hands every transport, and nothing else.
//
// NO SERVER ROUND TRIP HERE, unlike the contest-entry intent, and that absence
// removes a whole class of problem this flow does not have to solve. The
// challenge was minted by accounts#update_username BEFORE the engine modal
// called the hook, so there is no record for prepare to mint and therefore no
// outstanding-prepare ledger to retire on a rejected signature. A rename the
// user abandons leaves nothing behind but an unused signed token that expires.
window.tmPrepareUsernameRename = async function (ctx) {
  var challenge = ctx && ctx.challenge;
  if (!challenge) throw new Error('Could not save username');

  // base64 (what the server speaks) to base58 (what every wallet deeplink
  // protocol speaks, and the only shape that survives the journal).
  var bytes = Uint8Array.from(atob(challenge), function (c) { return c.charCodeAt(0); });
  return { transaction: window.SolanaStudio.walletTransport.base58.encode(bytes) };
};

// Complete: get the signed transaction onto the chain, wait for it, and — when
// nothing upstream is left alive — finish the engine's half of the flow too.
window.tmCompleteUsernameRename = async function (ctx, result, state) {
  var caller = window.tmUsernameRenameCaller;
  var onProgress = (caller && caller.onProgress) || function () {};
  var sig;

  // BOTH SEND STRATEGIES, and the branch is on what walletOps DECLARES rather
  // than on which field happens to be populated. `sendStrategy` is the gem's
  // own discriminator and it is set on every path out of run() and resume();
  // sniffing for a signature instead would read a redirect signTransaction
  // response that happened to carry one as a broadcast that never occurred.
  if (result && result.sendStrategy === 'wallet-broadcasts') {
    // The wallet already put this on the chain. There is nothing to send, and
    // trying to send it again would be a duplicate-signature error where the
    // honest answer is already in hand.
    sig = result.signature;
    if (!sig) {
      throw new Error('Your wallet reported that it broadcast the rename but did not return a signature.');
    }
    onProgress('Confirming…');
  } else {
    // We hold signed wire bytes and the broadcast is ours. This is every
    // desktop rename (runInline always answers app-broadcasts) and every
    // Phantom mobile rename (its send-side deeplink is deprecated, so
    // signingHop falls through to signTransaction).
    if (!result || !result.signedTransaction) {
      throw new Error('The wallet did not return a signed transaction, so the rename could not be broadcast.');
    }
    var signed = window.SolanaStudio.walletTransport.base58.decode(result.signedTransaction);
    onProgress('Confirming…');
    var conn = new solanaWeb3.Connection(renameRpcUrl(), 'confirmed');
    sig = await conn.sendRawTransaction(signed, { skipPreflight: true, maxRetries: 3 });
  }

  await window.tmRenamePollConfirmation(renameRpcUrl(), sig);

  // THE ENGINE'S HALF, run here ONLY when the engine cannot run it. See the
  // tmUsernameRenameCaller note above: on the inline transport the leveling
  // modal is still awaiting this promise and posts finalize_url itself with
  // whatever proof we return, exactly as it did before this migration. Posting
  // it here as well would confirm the same rename twice.
  if (caller) return { proof: sig };

  var resp = await window.tmRenameFetch(ctx.finalizeUrl, {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { 'X-CSRF-Token': renameCsrfToken(ctx), 'Content-Type': 'application/json', 'Accept': 'application/json' },
    body: JSON.stringify({ token: ctx.token, proof: sig })
  });
  if (!resp) throw new Error('Your session expired before the new username was saved.');

  var data = await resp.json();
  if ((data && data.status) !== 'saved') {
    throw new Error((data && data.message) || "Couldn't complete the step.");
  }

  // WHERE THE CALLBACK LANDS THE USER. studio-engine's phantom_callback view
  // navigates to result.value.redirect, falling back to '/'. The account page
  // is where the rename started and where the new name is visible, so it is
  // the honest destination — and naming it here is the only way the callback
  // can know, since it deliberately does not learn what the user was doing.
  //
  // WHAT IS LOST ON THIS TRANSPORT, stated rather than hidden: the
  // studio:username-saved celebration (confetti, the seeds fanout, the
  // quest-success swap in shared/_alpine_factories) fires from the engine
  // modal, which no longer exists. A redirect rename gets the saved name and no
  // party.
  //
  // THE MECHANISM NOW EXISTS — this note used to end "a separate piece of work",
  // and that work has since been done for the contest entry, which lost the same
  // three acts on the same leg. shared/_celebration_relay is the primitive: a
  // flow stashes its payload under its own KIND on the leg that cannot paint,
  // registers a painter for that kind, and the landing page drains it once. See
  // shared/_contest_entry_intent for a worked adoption, both halves of it.
  //
  // WHAT IS STILL TRUE is that a rename should not be blocked on it, so this leg
  // is deliberately left as it is rather than changed in passing. Adopting it is
  // two calls and one discriminator, not a rewrite.
  return { proof: sig, redirect: '/account' };
};
</script>
